This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:

The user's single request (verbatim intent, one message) was to add browser-based Google/GitHub sign-in next to the existing email/password auth in the `/app` (tui-gamepigeon) repo:
   - A new CLI command `gamepigeon auth oauth google` / `gamepigeon auth oauth github` opens the browser at the auth worker, "which rejects any other provider, checks that provider's credentials are configured, then redirects onward."
   - "On macOS route the link the way the desktop overlay already routes Arc/Dia links, a real tab via AppleScript, instead of a plain OS open."
   - "The CLI listens on 127.0.0.1:3210, falling back to a free port if busy, answers /auth/ping, and turns /auth/callback?token=... (tolerating a redirect that glues token onto an existing state param) into a saved session and a small HTML page, with an escaped reason shown on error or cancellation."
   - "The worker's GET /cli/oauth and GET /cli/handoff must each refuse a next target that isn't a plain http 127.0.0.1 or localhost URL before attaching a token."
   - "/cli/handoff hands the signed-in browser session back to that address, keeping its query, via an uncached self redirecting page, answering 401 with guidance when there's no session."
   - "From the CLI auth module export startOAuthCallbackServer(port), arcTabScript(bundleId, url) and isArcBundle(bundleId) so tests can drive the listener and Arc handling directly, following the exact shapes, trust rules, and cookie/error-page conventions pinned down in docs/oauth-handoff-contract.md."

Security-relevant constraints from the user/contract that must continue to apply: a session token may only ever be attached to a `next` URL that parses as `protocol === "http:"` with hostname `127.0.0.1` or `localhost`; that validation must happen before any session work (400 otherwise); the CLI listener has no nonce so `state` is accepted and ignored; error/cancellation reasons must be HTML-escaped in pages while the raw text goes into the Error; all `/auth/callback` responses set `Cache-Control: no-store`; OAuth client ids/secrets are Wrangler secrets, never committed.

